        <section class="panel content wide"><h2>Operating guide</h2><ol><li><strong>Register a WordPress site:</strong> Admin → WordPress Sites → add its public HTTPS URL; install the dashboard reporter ZIP in WordPress, configure its endpoint/token, then send a test report.</li><li><strong>Review or patch EC2:</strong> Admin → Dashboard → Sync EC2 updates. This reads SSM compliance and triggers AWS-RunPatchBaseline installation, which can reboot instances; verify the maintenance window before running it.</li><li><strong>Monitor uptime:</strong> Keep the Laravel scheduler running every minute. The uptime task runs every five minutes; WordPress checks use public HTTP(S) URLs and EC2 health uses AWS EC2 instance/system status in the configured region.</li><li><strong>Log a Config App change:</strong> Admin → Support Ticketing → create or update the ticket with status and start/finish times. Actor and before/after field changes are recorded in the ticket history and monthly report.</li><li><strong>Review access:</strong> Admin → User Management. Remove departed users promptly; administrators can manage integrations/settings, while viewers have monitoring access. All users require authenticator MFA.</li><li><strong>Backup recovery:</strong> Verify EC2 weekly backup jobs and WP Engine daily backup jobs in their provider consoles each month. Restore one backup in an isolated environment each quarter and record the date, asset, result and operator in the support ticket system.</li><li><strong>Monthly improvement:</strong> Review the recommendation and AWS Cost Explorer service breakdown; record the chosen action and measured result in a support ticket for next month's report.</li><li><strong>Report delivery:</strong> Admin → Reporting Settings → maintain the manager address. Treat the public report URL as a bearer secret; rotate it immediately if forwarded beyond its intended audience.</li></ol><p class="muted">Report limitations: availability is sampled, not continuous; missing probe periods are excluded from percentages. AWS cost data requires Cost Explorer permissions. Backup policies are reported assumptions, not provider verification. No vulnerability scanner is integrated.</p></section>
